<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>ISS reader · Sources and image credits</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css?v=03.4"><body><aside class="space-hub-bar" aria-label="Space Explorer topics"><a class="space-hub-home" href="../">← All space topics</a><div class="space-hub-links"><a href="../mars/">Mars</a><a href="../moon/">Earth’s Moon</a><a href="../iss/" aria-current="page">International Space Station</a><a href="../sun/">The Sun</a><a href="../constellations/">Constellations</a></div></aside><main class="credits-page"><a href="index.html">← Return to the reader</a><h1>Sources &amp; image credits</h1><p>All four photographs were downloaded from NASA and matched to their image records on October 30, 2026. No AI-generated photographs are used. The orbit and vocabulary illustrations are original teaching drawings.</p><article><h2>iss056e201302</h2><img src="assets/images/station.jpg" alt="The International Space Station with its long solar arrays above the blue, curved edge of Earth." style="max-width:420px;width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:contain"><p>The ISS above Earth, photographed from a departing Soyuz spacecraft by Expedition 56 crew members on October 4, 2018. Credit: NASA/Roscosmos.</p><p><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/international-space-station-as-of-oct-4-2018-5/">NASA image record</a> · <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/iss056e201302.jpg">NASA image file</a></p></article><article><h2>iss062e004933</h2><img src="assets/images/floating.jpg" alt="Astronaut Jessica Meir floats inside the Kibo laboratory with her hair raised and a round water sphere in front of her face." style="max-width:420px;width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:contain"><p>Jessica Meir observes a floating sphere of water inside the ISS Kibo laboratory, February 9, 2020. Credit: NASA.</p><p><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/flight-engineer-jessica-meir-observes-floating-sphere-of-water/">NASA image record</a> · <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/iss062e004933.jpg">NASA image file</a></p></article><article><h2>iss064e047074</h2><img src="assets/images/veggie.jpg" alt="Astronaut Soichi Noguchi holds a tray of leafy pak choi plants grown aboard the International Space Station for the Veggie investigation." style="max-width:420px;width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:contain"><p>JAXA astronaut Soichi Noguchi displays Extra Dwarf Pak Choi plants grown for the Veggie study aboard the ISS, March 26, 2021. Credit: NASA.</p><p><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/jaxa-astronaut-soichi-noguchi-works-veggie-experiment/">NASA image record</a> · <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/iss064e047074.jpg">NASA image file</a></p></article><article><h2>iss067e033745</h2><img src="assets/images/cupola.jpg" alt="Jessica Watkins floats in the Cupola, looking through its large windows at the bright blue and white Earth below." style="max-width:420px;width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:contain"><p>NASA astronaut Jessica Watkins looks toward Earth through the ISS Cupola windows, May 2022. Credit: NASA.</p><p><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/astronaut-jessica-watkins-enjoys-view-of-earth-below/">NASA image record</a> · <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/iss067e033745.jpg">NASA image file</a></p></article><h2>Science references</h2><p><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/international-space-station/space-station-facts-and-figures/">ISS Facts and Figures</a>: orbital period, sunrises, exercise, cooperation, continuous occupation.</p><p><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/centers-and-facilities/glenn/what-is-microgravity/">What Is Microgravity?</a>: gravity in orbit, speed, free fall and the orbit teaching diagram.</p><p><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/missions/station/iss-research/observing-our-planet-from-low-earth-orbit/">Observing Our Planet from Low Earth Orbit</a>: approximate 400 km altitude.</p><p><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/missions/station/iss-research/why-do-research-on-the-international-space-station/">Why Do Research on the ISS?</a>: research conditions and benefits.</p><h2>Globe illustration</h2><p>The globe uses <a href="https://github.com/nvkelso/natural-earth-vector/blob/master/geojson/ne_110m_land.geojson">Natural Earth 1:110m land data</a>, projected as a globe. <a href="https://www.naturalearthdata.com/about/terms-of-use/">Natural Earth data are in the public domain</a>. It is an original teaching illustration.</p><h2>Editorial notes</h2><p>Based on the supplied Issue 03 manuscript. Short paragraphs were grouped for translation controls. The print edition is edited for four-page balance, preserving the core science and activities. Home-language translations were checked for content alignment, terminology, numbers and causal meaning; independent native-speaking educator review remains recommended.</p><p>Photographs are historical views; the station configuration changes. NASA does not endorse this reader.</p></main></body></html>